          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "{\n \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\",\n \"name\": \"Codes.lean\",\n \"url\": \"https://uuidna.com/lean/Codes.lean\",\n \"inLanguage\": \"lean4\",\n \"variableMeasured\": {\n  \"@type\": \"PropertyValue\",\n  \"name\": \"theorems\",\n  \"value\": 9,\n  \"unitText\": \"theorem\"\n }\n}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-hamming_seven_four",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Hamming(7,4): 4 data bits + 3 parity bits = 7, carrying 2⁴ = 16 codewords — three redundant bits protect four."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "4 + 3 = 7 ∧ 2^4 = 16"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem hamming_seven_four : 4 + 3 = 7 ∧ 2^4 = 16 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-hamming_perfect_code",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Hamming(7,4) is a PERFECT code: each of the 16 codewords owns a sphere of 1 (itself) + 7 (single-bit flips) = 8, and 16 × 8 = 128 = 2⁷ — the spheres tile the whole 7-bit space exactly, no word wasted."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "16 * 8 = 128 ∧ 2^7 = 128"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem hamming_perfect_code : 16 * 8 = 128 ∧ 2^7 = 128 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-singleton_bound",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The minimum distance d = 3 satisfies the Singleton bound d ≤ n − k + 1 = 7 − 4 + 1 = 4 — no code can beat it, and Hamming sits one below."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "3 ≤ 7 - 4 + 1"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem singleton_bound : 3 ≤ 7 - 4 + 1 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-distance_three_corrects_one",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "A minimum distance of 3 corrects ⌊(d−1)/2⌋ = ⌊2/2⌋ = 1 error: any single flip lands strictly nearer its own codeword than any other."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(3 - 1) / 2 = 1"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem distance_three_corrects_one : (3 - 1) / 2 = 1 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-distance_three_detects_two",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The same distance-3 code DETECTS d − 1 = 2 errors — two flips can never reach another codeword, so they are always noticed (though not corrected)."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "3 - 1 = 2"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem distance_three_detects_two : 3 - 1 = 2 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-repetition_three_majority",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The (3,1) repetition code corrects one flip by MAJORITY: [1,1,1] with one bit flipped still shows two 1s, and 2·2 > 3 makes two a strict majority of three."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(([1,1,0].filter (fun x => x == 1)).length = 2) ∧ (2 * 2 > 3)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem repetition_three_majority : (([1,1,0].filter (fun x => x == 1)).length = 2) ∧ (2 * 2 > 3) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-xor_checksum_catches_flip",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "A linear XOR checksum catches any single flip: XOR is self-inverse, so flipping a word by d and re-checking recovers exactly d — (a ⊕ d) ⊕ a = d, for every a. The error cannot hide."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(List.range 8).all (fun a => lxor (lxor a 5) a == 5)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem xor_checksum_catches_flip : (List.range 8).all (fun a => lxor (lxor a 5) a == 5) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-codewords_sparse",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Correction needs room: 2⁴ = 16 codewords sit sparsely inside 2⁷ = 128 possible words (16 < 128) — the redundancy is exactly what lets a flipped word be traced back to its origin."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "2^4 < 2^7"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem codewords_sparse : 2^4 < 2^7 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-xor_checksum_is_involutive_over_every_nibble_pair",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE XOR CHECKSUM IS ITS OWN UNDOING, OVER ALL 256 PAIRS. Applying a key twice returns the message — (a XOR b) XOR b = a — which is why XOR is a checksum and a cipher at once, and why it is never secrecy on its own. The claim is decided for every one of the 16 × 16 = 256 four-bit pairs, using this wing’s own axiom-free lxor rather than Lean’s native Nat.xor, whose well-founded recursion is not kernel-only."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((List.range 16).all (fun a => (List.range 16).all (fun b => lxor (lxor a b) b == a))) ∧ (16 * 16 = 256)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem xor_checksum_is_involutive_over_every_nibble_pair : ((List.range 16).all (fun a => (List.range 16).all (fun b => lxor (lxor a b) b == a))) ∧ (16 * 16 = 256) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
